WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.530 --> 00:00:05.370
It's finally here: the first full-length trailer
for the hotly anticipated Netflix show The

00:00:05.370 --> 00:00:09.950
Witcher, based on Andrzej Sapkowski's book
series of the same name, which has famously

00:00:09.950 --> 00:00:13.059
been adapted into a collection of acclaimed
video games.

00:00:13.059 --> 00:00:16.770
Witcher showrunner Lauren Schmidt Hissrich
pulled the veil off the footage at the Lucca

00:00:16.770 --> 00:00:20.920
Comics and Games Convention in Lucca, Italy
on October 31st.

00:00:21.180 --> 00:00:25.040
All the major players of The Witcher appear
in this trailer, including, of course, Henry

00:00:25.040 --> 00:00:29.840
Cavill as Geralt of Rivia — the namesake
witcher who underwent a number of mutations

00:00:29.840 --> 00:00:34.210
and was trained from a very young age to develop
superhuman abilities to hunt monsters of all

00:00:34.210 --> 00:00:34.700
sorts.

00:00:35.120 --> 00:00:37.940
Apart from doing what trailers should do in
laying the groundwork for what the show is

00:00:37.940 --> 00:00:42.640
about and confirming its December 20th release
date, the first trailer for The Witcher includes

00:00:42.760 --> 00:00:47.390
several small details sure to make fans of
the property smile — and total Witcher newbies

00:00:47.390 --> 00:00:49.300
get excited about the upcoming series.

00:00:49.690 --> 00:00:53.320
In the first few seconds of the trailer for
The Witcher, the footage flashes to show an

00:00:53.340 --> 00:00:56.480
extreme close-up of a violet-colored eye — Yennefer's
eye.

00:00:56.680 --> 00:01:00.800
A skilled sorceress and the eventual love
of Geralt, Yennefer is described in canon

00:01:00.809 --> 00:01:05.680
as having "a pale triangular face, violet
eyes, and narrow, slightly contorted lips."

00:01:05.680 --> 00:01:09.729
Networks and showrunners have actually tried
— and failed — to bring violet-eyed characters

00:01:09.729 --> 00:01:10.860
to the small screen before.

00:01:11.160 --> 00:01:15.240
Within George R.R. Martin's Game of Thrones
novels, each member of the royal Targaryen

00:01:15.240 --> 00:01:20.260
family has purple eyes, ranging from pale
lilac to deep indigo, that create a contrast

00:01:20.260 --> 00:01:22.750
with their pale white, sometimes silvery hair.

00:01:22.750 --> 00:01:27.110
HBO's television adaptation of Martin's books
got the hair right but the show opted not

00:01:27.110 --> 00:01:29.160
to make the Targaryens' eyes violet.

00:01:29.160 --> 00:01:33.640
Not long after we're met with Yennefer's striking
purple gaze, we see something just as eye-catching

00:01:33.640 --> 00:01:37.560
in the trailer for The Witcher: a shot of
Geralt holding a metallic trinket in his blood-caked

00:01:37.560 --> 00:01:38.080
hand.

00:01:38.580 --> 00:01:41.920
One may assume that the object is Geralt's
own White Wolf medallion that he can tune

00:01:41.920 --> 00:01:44.620
to detect beasts nearby — but it isn't.

00:01:44.920 --> 00:01:49.460
In actuality, it's the brooch of Princess
Renfri, who's sort of a brutal, badass version

00:01:49.460 --> 00:01:50.760
of Snow White in the books.

00:01:51.000 --> 00:01:54.600
Later in the trailer, Geralt is shown riding
on his horse while another man walks beside

00:01:54.600 --> 00:01:55.120
him.

00:01:55.560 --> 00:01:59.020
That mystery man is Dandelion, a poet and
bard who offers quite a bit of comic relief

00:01:59.160 --> 00:02:00.160
in the games.

00:02:00.620 --> 00:02:04.260
In the Witcher canon, Dandelion becomes a
close friend of Geralt's after the two meet

00:02:04.260 --> 00:02:08.680
during a festival where Dandelion was trying
to evade some particularly nasty dudes.

00:02:08.940 --> 00:02:12.590
The shot of the two in the Witcher trailer
does feature scenery that feels similar to

00:02:12.590 --> 00:02:17.260
what's described in Sapkowski's short story
"The Edge of the World," which recounts Dandelion's

00:02:17.260 --> 00:02:21.730
first meeting with Geralt, so it seems likely
that the Netflix series will indeed detail

00:02:21.730 --> 00:02:22.960
the pair's initial meeting.

00:02:23.220 --> 00:02:26.660
Geralt of Rivia offers some powerful words
in the middle of the first full-length trailer

00:02:26.660 --> 00:02:29.240
— but they weren't written by the show's
creative team.

00:02:29.500 --> 00:02:33.940
The short speech he gives in the newly released
footage is actually taken almost verbatim

00:02:33.940 --> 00:02:37.500
from the short story "The Last Wish," which
precedes the main Witcher saga.

00:02:37.500 --> 00:02:38.500
Geralt says,

00:02:38.700 --> 00:02:39.700
"Evil is evil.

00:02:39.900 --> 00:02:42.460
Lesser, greater, middling, it's all the same."

00:02:42.700 --> 00:02:44.100
In the story, he goes on to say,

00:02:44.340 --> 00:02:46.460
"Proportions are negotiated, boundaries blurred.

00:02:46.720 --> 00:02:49.860
I'm not a pious hermit, I haven't done only
good in my life.

00:02:50.180 --> 00:02:54.040
But if I'm to choose between one evil and
another, then I prefer not to choose at all."

00:02:54.120 --> 00:02:58.760
And finally, diehard Witcher enthusiasts flipped
their lids over one particular moment in the

00:02:58.760 --> 00:03:02.900
series' trailer that casual fans and those
unfamiliar with the property were probably

00:03:02.900 --> 00:03:07.689
a bit confused by: Geralt chillin' in the
bathtub, his medallion around his neck, candles

00:03:07.689 --> 00:03:09.580
lit behind him, and Yennefer by his side.

00:03:10.070 --> 00:03:14.500
This isn't Netflix's attempt at using Henry
Cavill's sex appeal to attract audiences — it's

00:03:14.500 --> 00:03:19.269
actually a near-exact replica of a cutscene
from The Witcher 3: Wild Hunt, the CD Projekt

00:03:19.269 --> 00:03:21.280
Red video game released in 2015.

00:03:21.560 --> 00:03:26.240
That game showed Geralt in supreme relaxation
mode, sitting in a bathtub with his feet sticking

00:03:26.240 --> 00:03:26.740
out of the basin.

00:03:27.100 --> 00:03:31.579
Bathtub Geralt became an instant meme and
was even turned into a "marvelously detailed,

00:03:31.580 --> 00:03:34.000
hand-painted statuette" issued by Dark Horse.

00:03:34.380 --> 00:03:36.040
"Don't judge me."

00:03:36.129 --> 00:03:39.900
He may not poke his toes out of the water
in the scene on Netflix's series, instead

00:03:39.900 --> 00:03:44.269
opting to be a little more demure during his
down time, but Bathtub Geralt is unequivocally

00:03:44.269 --> 00:03:45.100
here to stay.

00:03:45.380 --> 00:03:48.980
All the more reason to watch The Witcher when
it hits Netflix on December 20th.

